import sys
p=sys.argv[1]; t=open(p).read()
def ins_after(anchor, text):
    global t
    assert anchor in t, anchor[:70]; t=t.replace(anchor, anchor+text, 1)
def rep(a,b):
    global t
    assert a in t, a[:70]; t=t.replace(a,b,1)

ins_after("this is beyond what pin-limited systems can deliver for random access~\\cite{ahn2015tesseract}.",
" Caches do not close this gap. Piccolo measures that more than 90\\% of the bytes a cache fetches during BFS are evicted without ever being used, because one useful 4--8\\,B vertex arrives inside a 64\\,B burst~\\cite{shin2025piccolo}. When the useful fraction is that low, adding bandwidth or capacity to a conventional hierarchy gives diminishing returns. Architects therefore either reduce the distance data travels or increase the useful fraction of each transfer.")
rep("Graph neural network accelerators, GPU-only systems, and near-storage graph processing are outside our scope.",
"Graph neural network accelerators, GPU-only systems, and near-storage graph processing are outside our scope.\n\nThe remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\\ref{sec:background} introduces the workload model, the placement taxonomy, and our survey method. Sections~\\ref{sec:pim} and~\\ref{sec:dsa} review the PIM and DSA lineages, and Section~\\ref{sec:hybrid} covers the hybrid designs that combine them. Section~\\ref{sec:analysis} compares the camps on throughput, energy, bandwidth, evidence quality, cost, and the assumptions that decide between them. Section~\\ref{sec:critical} evaluates security, dependability, and predictability, Section~\\ref{sec:open} lists open challenges, and the final section summarizes our conclusions.")
ins_after("which unbalances both partitions and crossbar mappings~\\cite{zheng2020spara,cai2024pimpam}.",
"\n\nTwo execution-model choices recur and affect hardware design directly. The first is \\emph{synchronization}. Bulk-synchronous designs process all active vertices of an iteration before starting the next, as Graphicionado does~\\cite{ham2016graphicionado}. Asynchronous and event-driven designs propagate updates as soon as they are produced. JetStream builds on such an event-driven accelerator, which its authors report achieved up to $6\\times$ over a bulk-synchronous design~\\cite{rahman2021jetstream}, and GraphQ's multi-node model tolerates bounded staleness~\\cite{zhuo2019graphq}. The second is \\emph{direction}. Push-based execution writes updates into neighbours and therefore creates scattered writes that PIM can absorb in place. Pull-based execution reads from neighbours and does not map as naturally onto PIM. CoGraf, for example, evaluates push-only variants for this reason~\\cite{yenimol2026cograf}. GraphDynS summarizes the resulting difficulties as three irregularities: \\emph{workload} irregularity (unequal edges per thread), \\emph{traversal} irregularity (unpredictable edges per iteration), and \\emph{update} irregularity (conflicting writes to shared vertices)~\\cite{yan2019graphdyns}. Reported metrics vary across papers. Most report speedup and energy relative to a baseline; some report traversed edges per second (TEPS) or TEPS per watt~\\cite{lee2022graphwave}. This variety is one reason why direct comparison across camps is difficult.")
