rep("The hybrids matter for the research question in two ways.",
"The hybrids also respond to a cost argument. Piccolo deliberately avoids placing arithmetic units in DRAM, stating that doing so is expensive and that supporting multiple operation types there is impractical~\\cite{shin2025piccolo}. FALA notes that commercial DRAM-PIM products with parallel arithmetic units have been reported to add up to 50\\% area overhead to the DRAM die. It also points out that many earlier PIM proposals assumed separate logic dies with serial interconnects, which do not map onto monolithic DRAM-PIM~\\cite{shin2025fala}. The hybrids therefore place the minimum function in memory (scatter-gather, fine-grained column selection, simple reduction) and keep control and reuse on the processor side.\n\nThe hybrids matter for the research question in two ways.")
rep("\\begin{table*}[t]\n\\caption{Representative designs",
"""\\subsection{Cost, deployability, and programmability}
The camps also differ in what they require from the rest of the system. A DSA is a processor-side component. It can be an ASIC, a unit attached to each core~\\cite{mukkara2018hats,zhao2022tdgraph}, or an FPGA on an HBM card~\\cite{chen2022regraph}, and memory needs no change. PIM requires new memory parts: HMC logic layers~\\cite{ahn2015tesseract}, DIMM buffer-chip logic~\\cite{talati2022ndminer}, ReRAM crossbars~\\cite{song2018graphr}, or modified DRAM dies whose area cost is significant~\\cite{shin2025fala}. Of the PIM substrates surveyed, only UPMEM has been evaluated as a commercially available product~\\cite{gomezluna2022prim}. The hybrids are designed to keep in-memory changes small~\\cite{shin2025piccolo,shin2025fala}.

Programmability follows the same gradient. DSAs keep a single shared address space and a vertex-programming interface that existing frameworks already target~\\cite{ham2016graphicionado}. Programmable PnM cores (Tesseract, UPMEM) also accept general code, but they push partitioning, data placement, and communication onto the programmer or the runtime~\\cite{ahn2015tesseract,cai2024pimpam,barkhordar2025alphapim}. ALPHA-PIM shows that the best partitioning format depends on input density and dataset, which is a tuning burden absent from DSAs~\\cite{barkhordar2025alphapim}. Analog crossbars accept only SpMV-expressible kernels~\\cite{song2018graphr}, and fine-grained PIM exposes new instruction types that compilers must target~\\cite{yenimol2026cograf}. In short, the deeper the compute sits, the more of the graph's irregularity is handed back to software.

\\begin{table*}[t]
\\caption{Representative designs""")
rep("The architectural reason is straightforward. A DSA keeps the trust boundary at the memory controller, where conventional encryption applies, whereas PIM moves computation inside the boundary where plaintext must be processed.",
"The architectural reason is straightforward. A DSA keeps the trust boundary at the memory controller, where conventional encryption applies, whereas PIM moves computation inside the boundary where plaintext must be processed. Graph workloads sharpen the problem because the access pattern itself encodes the graph. A traversal's sequence of rows touched reveals adjacency and degree, so row-buffer timing channels of the kind IMPACT demonstrates could leak structural information, not merely data values. This is our inference: IMPACT's side channel targets other applications, and no surveyed paper has evaluated graph-structure leakage. MGX's efficiency, conversely, depends on the accelerator knowing its access pattern in advance~\\cite{hua2022mgx}. Data-dependent traversals and incremental updates are exactly where that assumption weakens.")
rep("Multi-tenant interference between concurrent queries has been studied only as a throughput problem in software~\\cite{yin2023glign}.",
"Multi-tenant interference between concurrent queries has been studied only as a throughput problem in software~\\cite{yin2023glign}. Some DSA structures are more predictable by construction. A scratchpad has fixed access latency, unlike a cache whose hit rate depends on the graph~\\cite{ham2016graphicionado}. Asynchronous and event-driven execution, on the other hand, makes the order of updates, and with it the iteration count, depend on timing~\\cite{rahman2021jetstream,zhuo2019graphq}. For incremental DSAs, latency is proportional to the size of the affected region, not of the graph~\\cite{rahman2021jetstream,zhao2022tdgraph}. This is favourable on average but unbounded in the worst case, since one update can affect the whole graph.")
